Q: Longest Non-Overlapping Repeated Substring using Suffix Tree/Array (Algorithm Only) A: <p>There is a fairly simple function for computing the longest non-overlapping common prefix for a &quot;block&quot; of overlapping common prefixes that uses the suffix array and lcp array.  Set the length of the longest common prefix to 0 and traverse through the suffix array.  If an element of the lcp array does not have overlapping suffixes, compare the lpc array value to the longest value found so far, and set the longest value if that lcp array value is larger than the previous longest value.  Otherwise, when the suffixes are overlapping, go to the end of the overlapping &quot;block&quot; of suffixes that contains the longest overlapping prefix, call that function, and check the return value to see if it is larger than the previous longest vlaue, and skip the rest of the overlapping suffixes.  Also, save the suffix array index of the longest prefix as you go if you want the location of the longest prefix.</p>
<p>This will run in O(N) time since the while loop in the function below will not have more iterations than the number of overlapping suffixes for which the length check can be skipped in the main program.</p>
<p>Disclaimer:  I just created this function because I couldn't find a simple and obvious answer on the internet for a similar need in a program I am developing.  While it works on my test files, it is possible I am missing something.  The function should only be called using the overlapping suffix at the &quot;long&quot; end of the suffix array overlap block  - it may not return accurate values for other suffixes.</p>
<p>As mentioned, the function is fairly simple.  It finds the lcp index for the longest overlapping suffix, determines the direction to move through the block of overlapping suffixes, finds the overlap distance between the suffixes, calculates the half span (lcp length + abs(distance)).  Then it moves through the overlap block until the overlap distance changes (another non-overlapping suffix has ended the overlap block) or the prefix is no longer than half the span.  The it sets the length as appropriate for what it found on that side of the block.  Lastly, it checks the original end of the block for a longer (non-overlapping) match than what it found on the other side of the block.</p>
<pre><code>int32_t find_max_non_overlap_length(int32_t *suffix_array, int32_t *lcp_array,
    int32_t sa_index) {
  int32_t lcp_index = sa_index + (lcp_array[sa_index] &lt; lcp_array[sa_index + 1]);
  int8_t dir = (suffix_array[lcp_index - 1] &gt; suffix_array[lcp_index]) ? -1 : 1;
  int32_t distance = suffix_array[lcp_index] - suffix_array[lcp_index - 1];
  int32_t half_span = (lcp_array[lcp_index] + dir * distance) &gt;&gt; 1;
  int32_t j = dir;
  while ((lcp_array[lcp_index] == lcp_array[lcp_index + j] + distance * j)
      &amp;&amp; (lcp_array[lcp_index + j] &gt; half_span))
    j += dir;
  int32_t length;
  if (lcp_array[lcp_index + j] &lt; lcp_array[lcp_index + j - dir])
    length = lcp_array[lcp_index + j];
  else
    length = lcp_array[lcp_index + j - dir];
  if ((length &lt; distance * j) &amp;&amp; (distance * j &lt;= half_span))
    length = distance * j;
  if (lcp_array[lcp_index - dir] &gt; length) {
    if (lcp_array[lcp_index - dir] &lt;= lcp_array[lcp_index])
      length = lcp_array[lcp_index - dir];
    else
      length = lcp_array[lcp_index];
  }
  return(length);
}
</code></pre>
